#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Download one file over https and keep it only if its SHA-256 equals the pinned value (plan step P0.11.5).

Usage: fetch-pinned <https-url> <sha256> <destination>

Builds bring files in from the network through this program and nothing else; the pin gate refuses any other
download in a file a build executes.

How a run goes. The body lands in a hidden scratch file next to the destination, hashed and size-limited as it
arrives, and is renamed over the destination only once the hash checks out. Whatever already sits at the
destination is dealt with before anything else is written. That first change to the destination moves whatever sits
at that name to a scratch name, or deletes it when the move is impossible or the run fails before reaching it. That old
file is reused, with no download, only if a fresh copy of it hashes to the pin; otherwise it and its copy are gone
before the download starts. Until that first change the program only reads, so a run stopped by any means at that
stage finds the destination untouched. Afterward the destination name is, at every moment, either vacant or holding
a checked file, even if the process is killed. The price is a short gap in which a good existing file is away from
its name while it is checked. One deadline of 15 minutes bounds the request, any redirects and the body.

Outcome and exit status. A run turns final at its commit point: the checked file is in place, interrupts are held,
the deadline is switched off, and one store marks the run as placed. Before that store the cleanup undoes the
placement; after it, nothing can. Interrupts arriving during the commit or anywhere in the cleanup wait instead of
raising, so a second Ctrl-C cannot stop a rollback half way, and each cleanup step runs even when an earlier step
failed. Hence a status of 1 always means no file was written, and a status of 0 always means one was. A waiting
interrupt is discarded when the run failed anyway; after a placement the command line still reports success, while
a library call raises the interrupt once the caller's handlers are back. Python runs signal handlers between
bytecodes, so the cleanup's first lines soak up the one interrupt and the one deadline signal that could arrive
just as it starts.

Process-wide state. Only the command-line entry point changes it for good: it installs the last-resort report, and
from the commit point to exit an interrupt is merely noted, so the status reported is the status on disk. A library
call, or main used in process, returns the interrupt and alarm handlers and installs no report. For the run, the
program sits in front of whatever Python-level SIGINT handler the caller has: while the download can still be stopped,
each interrupt goes through to that handler, which can raise only once, and one that comes while the handler is
still busy is passed on as soon as it returns; during the commit and the cleanup it is noted instead, and a caller's
own handler receives it once the cleanup is over. Should that handler install a different one, the program puts
itself in front of the new choice and hands that back at the end. An ignored interrupt stays ignored. The deadline
needs the alarm handler and the real-time timer, so a caller already running that timer, or whose alarm handler was
installed outside Python (one Python cannot put back), is turned away before anything changes; a timer that starts
during that check shows up in the program's own swap, which puts it back and fails the run. Both stay the run's
until it ends: whatever a callback the caller passes does to them meanwhile, the end of the run stops the timer and
reinstates the handler it began with, and such a callback can only give up the deadline, never inherit it. A timer or
handler that cannot be returned afterward raises StateNotRestored rather than passing silently, and the outcome of
the run stands; after a placement the command line keeps status 0 and prints the problem as one warning line.

Output. Every line printed is printable ASCII. Any other character, whether from a file name, a system error
message or anything a server sent back, appears as an escape, so no input can split or fake a log line. Every exception,
an interrupt included, ends as a single such line; the last-resort report prints one line and no chained text.

Permissions. Only the mode bits carry over: a checked copy of an existing file keeps its mode, but not its ACLs,
flags or extended attributes; a caller that needs those applies them after placement.

Threat model. Covered: an untrusted network, and a destination holding an old, truncated or wrong file. Not covered:
another process writing into the destination folder during the run, since such a process already has the build's
trust. The cleanup deletes a scratch name, or a file this run put at the destination, only while that name still
holds that exact file; each cleanup step runs regardless of earlier failures, and any name left behind is listed
in the refusal.

Inputs. The URL must be https, name a host, and carry no user name or password; redirects are followed only to
https. The digest is 64 hex digits. The destination must lie inside the working folder (an absolute path may reach
it through the shell's $PWD spelling when that resolves to the same place); every folder on its path must exist and
be a real folder, not a link, checked one level at a time and kept open for the write; the destination itself is
either missing or a regular file; it is valid UTF-8 without control characters, it plainly names a file (no trailing
slash, no empty, . or .. segment, though a leading ./ is fine), and its name is short enough for the scratch name
placed next to it.

Exit status. 0: placed. 1: refused, because the download failed or ran past its deadline, the bytes went over the
cap or hashed to something else, the run was interrupted, or the program could not clear the destination or its
scratch files. 2: malformed arguments. A status of 1 or 2 leaves nothing written, apart from any name the refusal
reports as not removed.
"""
import hashlib
import http.client
import ipaddress
import math
import os
import re
import secrets
import signal
import socket
import stat
import sys
import threading
import urllib.error
import urllib.parse
import urllib.request

MAX_BYTES = 512 * 1024 * 1024
TIMEOUT_SECONDS = 120
DEADLINE_SECONDS = 900
CHUNK = 1024 * 1024
HEX64 = re.compile(r"[0-9A-Fa-f]{64}")
PRINTABLE = re.compile(r"[\x21-\x7e]+")
CONTROL = re.compile(r"[\x00-\x1f\x7f-\x9f]")
LABEL = re.compile(r"(?!-)[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,63}(?<!-)")


class Refused(Exception):
    """The file was not placed."""


class Malformed(Refused):
    """The arguments are wrong; nothing was fetched."""


class TooLarge(Refused):
    """The bytes passed the cap."""


class StateNotRestored(Exception):
    """The run ended, placed or refused as its outcome records, but a timer or handler the helper changed could not
    be put back, so the caller learns of it instead of inheriting it silently."""


class HttpsOnlyRedirect(urllib.request.HTTPRedirectHandler):
    def redirect_request(self, req, fp, code, msg, headers, newurl):
        try:
            check_url(newurl)  # the same rule as the first URL: https, a host, no user name or password
        except Malformed as error:
            raise Refused(f"a redirect target is not allowed: {error}") from None
        return super().redirect_request(req, fp, code, msg, headers, newurl)


def https_only_opener():
    return urllib.request.build_opener(HttpsOnlyRedirect)


def check_url(url):
    """Reject, before anything is downloaded, any URL that urllib could not send or resolve exactly as written: text
    outside printable ASCII (spaces, control characters, letters beyond ASCII), an opening bracket with no closing
    one, a port outside 1 to 65535 or not numeric, and a host that is neither an address nor a well-formed name."""
    if not isinstance(url, str) or not PRINTABLE.fullmatch(url):
        raise Malformed("the URL must be printable ASCII, with no space or control character")
    try:
        parts = urllib.parse.urlsplit(url)
        host, port = parts.hostname, parts.port
    except ValueError as error:
        raise Malformed(f"the URL cannot be read: {error}") from None
    if port == 0:
        raise Malformed("the URL's port must be 1 to 65535")
    if parts.scheme.lower() != "https" or not host:
        raise Malformed("the URL must be https with a host")
    if not valid_host(host):
        raise Malformed("the URL's host must be an address or a well-formed host name")
    if parts.username is not None or parts.password is not None:
        raise Malformed("the URL must not carry a user name or password")


def valid_host(host):
    """True for an address, or for a name made of dot-separated labels (each 1 to 63 letters, digits, underscores or
    hyphens not at either end, 253 characters at most overall, with one optional trailing dot). A host the system's
    own address parser reads as an address (hex, octal or short forms such as 0x7f000001, 017.0.0.1 or 127.1) must
    be written as one, and a name's last label is not all digits, since the resolver would read it as an address
    too. inet_aton parses locally and looks nothing up."""
    try:
        ipaddress.ip_address(host)
        return True
    except ValueError:
        pass
    labels = (host[:-1] if host.endswith(".") else host).split(".")
    try:
        socket.inet_aton(".".join(labels))
        return False
    except OSError:
        pass
    return (0 < len(".".join(labels)) <= 253 and all(LABEL.fullmatch(label) for label in labels)
            and not labels[-1].isdigit())


FOLDER = os.O_RDONLY | os.O_DIRECTORY | os.O_NOFOLLOW


def open_destination(dest, root):
    """Return (folder handle, file name) for dest. Each folder below root is opened in turn, refusing links, and all
    later work goes through the last handle; a link, or a folder replaced once the check is done, therefore cannot
    redirect the write outside root. The destination has to be missing or a regular file."""
    root = os.path.realpath(root)
    if not dest:
        raise Malformed("the destination must name a file")
    if CONTROL.search(dest):
        raise Malformed("the destination must not hold a control character")
    try:
        dest.encode("utf-8")
    except UnicodeEncodeError:
        raise Malformed("the destination must be valid UTF-8 text") from None
    pieces = (dest[2:] if dest.startswith("./") else dest).split("/")
    if any(piece in ("", ".", "..") for piece in (pieces[1:] if os.path.isabs(dest) else pieces)):
        raise Malformed("the destination must be a plain file path: no trailing slash, and no empty, . or .. segment")
    path = os.path.normpath(dest if os.path.isabs(dest) else os.path.join(root, dest))
    prefixes = [root]
    shell = os.path.normpath(os.environ.get("PWD") or ".")  # the shell's spelling of the same folder, if it is one
    if os.path.isabs(shell) and os.path.realpath(shell) == root:
        prefixes.append(shell)
    inside = [prefix for prefix in prefixes if path != prefix and os.path.commonpath([prefix, path]) == prefix]
    if not inside:  # whole segments compared: / as the folder holds any path; /a/work does not contain /a/workshop
        raise Malformed("the destination is outside the folder the helper runs in")
    parts = os.path.relpath(path, inside[0]).split(os.sep)
    if any(part in ("", ".", "..") for part in parts):
        raise Malformed("the destination must be a plain path to a file")
    try:
        folder = os.open(root, os.O_RDONLY | os.O_DIRECTORY)
    except OSError as error:
        raise Malformed(f"the folder the helper runs in cannot be opened: {error.strerror}") from None
    try:
        for part in parts[:-1]:
            try:
                inner = os.open(part, FOLDER, dir_fd=folder)
            except OSError:
                raise Malformed("every folder in the destination path must exist and be a real folder, not a link")
            close_quietly(folder)
            folder = inner
        try:
            longest = os.fpathconf(folder, "PC_NAME_MAX")
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            longest = 255
        if len(temporary_name(parts[-1]).encode()) > longest:  # its temporary name must fit beside it
            raise Malformed("the destination's name is too long for the temporary name the helper puts beside it")
        try:
            info = os.stat(parts[-1], dir_fd=folder, follow_symlinks=False)
        except FileNotFoundError:
            return folder, parts[-1]
        if not stat.S_ISREG(info.st_mode):
            raise Malformed("the destination must be missing or a regular file; a link, folder or device is refused")
        return folder, parts[-1]
    except BaseException:
        close_quietly(folder)
        raise


class Deadline:
    """The run's single deadline. It raises once at most, and only while armed. If the time runs out before arming,
    arming raises it; after disarming, running out has no effect, so a late alarm can neither interrupt the cleanup
    nor turn a checked placement into a failure."""

    def __init__(self):
        self.armed, self.passed = False, False

    def expired(self, signum, frame):
        self.passed = True
        if self.armed:
            self.armed = False
            raise Refused("the fetch passed its deadline")

    def arm(self):
        self.armed = True
        if self.passed:
            self.armed = False
            raise Refused("the fetch passed its deadline")

    def disarm(self):
        self.armed = False


class Outcome:
    """The run's record of its one commit point: placed is set by a single store once the verified file is in place,
    and the cleanup and main read it. A record belongs to one run: one already marked placed is refused."""

    def __init__(self):
        self.placed = False


class Interrupts:
    """How the run treats SIGINT. While armed, an interrupt behaves as the caller set it up: Python's default handler
    raises KeyboardInterrupt, and a caller's own handler is called, with the signal mask exactly as the caller left
    it. An interrupt that lands while that handler is still busy is passed to it the moment it returns. Whichever
    handler raises disarms the holder, so it can raise only once. Once held, an interrupt is only noted, so the
    commit and the cleanup always run to their end. The holder sits in front of any Python-level handler for the run;
    an ignored interrupt, or one left to the operating system, is not touched."""

    def __init__(self):
        self.armed, self.held, self.installed, self.previous = True, False, False, None
        self.busy, self.again = False, False

    def fired(self, signum, frame):
        if not self.armed:
            self.held = True
            return
        if self.busy:  # the caller's handler is still running: it gets this one as soon as it returns
            self.again = True
            return
        while True:
            previous = self.previous
            if previous is None or previous is signal.default_int_handler:
                self.armed = False
                raise KeyboardInterrupt
            if previous is signal.SIG_IGN:
                return
            if previous is signal.SIG_DFL:  # the caller asked for the system's action: give it back and let it act
                signal.signal(signal.SIGINT, signal.SIG_DFL)
                self.installed = False
                signal.raise_signal(signal.SIGINT)
                return
            # The caller's handler runs with the mask as the caller left it, so whatever it blocks stays blocked.
            self.busy = True
            try:
                previous(signum, frame)
            except BaseException:
                self.armed = False  # it raised, so the run is ending: from here on interrupts wait for the cleanup
                if self.again:
                    self.again, self.held = False, True
                raise
            finally:
                try:
                    self.adopt_guarded()  # it may have installed another handler: front that one from now on
                finally:
                    self.busy = False
            if not self.again:
                return
            self.again = False  # one more arrived while it ran: hand it over now, not at the end of the run

    def adopt(self):
        """If something other than this holder is now SIGINT's handler, the caller replaced it: remember the
        caller's latest choice and put the holder back in front of it. A handler set outside Python reads as None
        and could never be handed back once replaced, so the holder steps aside and leaves that one in place."""
        current = signal.getsignal(signal.SIGINT)
        if not self.installed or current == self.fired:
            return
        if current is None:
            self.installed, self.previous = False, None
            return
        self.previous = current
        signal.signal(signal.SIGINT, self.fired)

    def install(self):
        current = signal.getsignal(signal.SIGINT)
        if callable(current):
            self.previous = current
            self.installed = True  # first, so a release after an interrupt inside the next call still restores
            signal.signal(signal.SIGINT, self.fired)

    def adopt_guarded(self):
        """Adopt with SIGINT blocked, so an interrupt cannot reach a new handler in the moment between reading it
        and fronting it. No caller code runs inside that block, so lifting it undoes only the holder's own."""
        if not self.installed or signal.getsignal(signal.SIGINT) == self.fired:
            return
        blocked = signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_BLOCK, {signal.SIGINT})
        try:
            self.adopt()
        finally:
            if signal.SIGINT not in blocked:
                signal.pthread_sigmask(signal.SIG_UNBLOCK, {signal.SIGINT})

    def hold(self):
        self.armed = False
        self.adopt_guarded()

    def release(self):
        """Give the caller back its latest SIGINT handler. An interrupt noted while held is sent again unless that
        handler is Python's default, whose case the run decides (fetch_pinned), or ignores interrupts."""
        if not self.installed:
            return
        current = signal.getsignal(signal.SIGINT)
        if current == self.fired:
            signal.signal(signal.SIGINT, self.previous)
        elif current is not None:  # the caller's latest choice is already in place: leave it there
            self.previous = current
        self.installed = False
        previous = self.previous
        if self.held and previous is not signal.default_int_handler and previous is not signal.SIG_IGN:
            self.held = False
            signal.raise_signal(signal.SIGINT)


def fetch_pinned(url, digest, dest, open_url=None, root=None, max_bytes=MAX_BYTES, deadline_seconds=DEADLINE_SECONDS,
                 outcome=None, interrupts=None):
    """Put url's body at dest if its SHA-256 equals digest, and raise Refused if not. An existing file at dest is
    reused without a download only when a fresh copy of it hashes correctly; anything else there is cleared first,
    so after argument checking, dest holds only checked content whether the call returns or raises. The deadline
    starts once the arguments pass and covers hashing an existing file, connecting, redirects, the body and the
    rename; only the closing cleanup runs without it, so the cleanup is never cut off. outcome is a record the
    caller can read for the commit point. interrupts is a holder the caller supplies (main does); without one, the
    call manages interrupts itself for the duration, returns the handler at the end, and re-raises a held interrupt
    only if the file was placed. Malformed: a deadline other than a positive finite number, a byte cap that is
    negative or not an integer, and a holder or record reused from another run. Every timer and handler this call
    changes is returned on every path, or StateNotRestored names the one that could not be; the call turns away a
    caller whose real-time timer is running or whose alarm handler Python cannot put back, before changing anything."""
    check_url(url)
    if not HEX64.fullmatch(digest or ""):
        raise Malformed("the digest must be 64 hex digits")
    if threading.current_thread() is not threading.main_thread():
        raise Malformed("the helper runs its deadline on the main thread only")
    if outcome is not None and outcome.placed:
        raise Malformed("the outcome record is from an earlier run; each run needs a fresh one")
    if interrupts is not None and (not interrupts.armed or interrupts.held):
        raise Malformed("the interrupt holder is from an earlier run; each run needs a fresh one")
    if (isinstance(deadline_seconds, bool) or not isinstance(deadline_seconds, (int, float))
            or not math.isfinite(deadline_seconds) or deadline_seconds <= 0):
        raise Malformed("the deadline must be a positive, finite number of seconds")  # 0 would cancel the timer
    if isinstance(max_bytes, bool) or not isinstance(max_bytes, int) or max_bytes < 0:
        raise Malformed("the byte cap must be a whole number, 0 or more")
    alarm = signal.getsignal(signal.SIGALRM)
    if alarm is None:
        raise Malformed("the alarm handler was set outside Python, which cannot put it back, so the deadline cannot "
                        "borrow it")
    if signal.getitimer(signal.ITIMER_REAL)[0] > 0:
        raise Malformed("a real-time timer is already running, and the helper's deadline needs it")
    state = outcome if outcome is not None else Outcome()
    holder = interrupts if interrupts is not None else Interrupts()
    deadline, folder, unrestored, deferred, timer = Deadline(), None, [], None, None
    try:
        if interrupts is None:
            holder.install()
        folder, name = open_destination(dest, root if root is not None else os.getcwd())
        signal.signal(signal.SIGALRM, deadline.expired)
        timer = signal.setitimer(signal.ITIMER_REAL, deadline_seconds)
        if timer[0] > 0:  # a timer started after the check: the final block puts it back as it was
            raise Refused("a real-time timer started while the helper was taking it, so it was put back")
        place(url, digest.lower(), folder, name, open_url, max_bytes, deadline, state, holder)
    except (Exception, KeyboardInterrupt):
        raise
    except BaseException as error:  # an exit request in flight: nothing below may replace it
        deferred = error
        raise
    finally:
        # The steps below each run even if an earlier one failed. Python delivers signals between bytecodes, so these
        # repeated attempts soak up the single interrupt and the single deadline alarm that might still arrive.
        try:
            holder.hold()
            deadline.disarm()
        except (Exception, KeyboardInterrupt):
            pass
        except BaseException as error:  # not the cleanup's to drop: raised once every step has run
            deferred = deferred or error
        try:
            holder.hold()
            deadline.disarm()
        except (Exception, KeyboardInterrupt):
            pass
        except BaseException as error:  # not the cleanup's to drop: raised once every step has run
            deferred = deferred or error
        try:
            holder.hold()
            deadline.disarm()
        except (Exception, KeyboardInterrupt):
            pass
        except BaseException as error:  # not the cleanup's to drop: raised once every step has run
            deferred = deferred or error
        # Each restoration is tried twice, and one that still fails is named after the others have run. The order keeps
        # every alarm with its own handler: the helper's timer is cancelled, then the caller's handler goes back, and
        # only then is a timer the helper's swap displaced re-armed, and only if that handler is back.
        try:
            signal.setitimer(signal.ITIMER_REAL, 0)
        except Exception:
            try:
                signal.setitimer(signal.ITIMER_REAL, 0)
            except Exception as error:
                unrestored.append(f"the real-time timer ({described(error)})")
        handler_back = True
        try:
            signal.signal(signal.SIGALRM, alarm)
        except Exception:
            try:
                signal.signal(signal.SIGALRM, alarm)
            except Exception as error:
                handler_back = False
                unrestored.append(f"the alarm handler ({described(error)})")
        if timer is not None and timer[0] > 0:  # a timer the swap displaced
            if not handler_back:
                unrestored.append("the caller's real-time timer (not re-armed: its handler could not be put back)")
            else:
                try:
                    signal.setitimer(signal.ITIMER_REAL, *timer)
                except Exception:
                    try:
                        signal.setitimer(signal.ITIMER_REAL, *timer)
                    except Exception as error:
                        unrestored.append(f"the caller's real-time timer ({described(error)})")
        if folder is not None:
            try:
                close_quietly(folder)
            except Exception:
                pass
        if interrupts is None:
            try:
                holder.release()
            except Exception:
                try:
                    holder.release()
                except Exception as error:
                    unrestored.append(f"the interrupt handler ({described(error)})")
        if deferred is not None:
            raise deferred  # an exit request outranks a report the caller can no longer act on
        if unrestored:
            raise StateNotRestored(f"could not restore {', '.join(unrestored)}")
        if interrupts is None and state.placed and holder.held:  # the caller's handler is back: raise it now
            holder.held = False
            raise KeyboardInterrupt
    return dest


def close_quietly(handle):
    """Close a handle, ignoring errors; every close in the program goes through here. Closing cannot change the
    result: by then the data was flushed and checked, or the run has already failed, so a close error never swaps
    one failure for another and never turns a success into a failure."""
    try:
        os.close(handle)
    except OSError:
        pass


def remove(folder, name):
    """The one way the helper removes a name. A name already gone is fine; any other failure is a refusal that names
    what was left, so exit 1 never hides a leftover."""
    try:
        os.unlink(name, dir_fd=folder)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        pass
    except OSError as error:
        raise Refused(f"could not remove {name}: {error.strerror or error}") from None


def temporary_name(name):
    """The one shape of every temporary name: hidden, beside the destination, random, and marked as partial."""
    return f".{name}.{secrets.token_hex(8)}.part"


def identity(info):
    return info.st_dev, info.st_ino


def remove_own(folder, name, own):
    """Remove name only while it still holds the file this run made or read (own is its device and inode). A name
    whose file is not known yet (own is None: the run was cut off between creating it and recording it) is removed
    as it stands: in that instant it holds this run's new file unless another process raced it, which the threat
    model leaves out."""
    if own is not None:
        try:
            if identity(os.stat(name, dir_fd=folder, follow_symlinks=False)) != own:
                return
        except FileNotFoundError:
            return
    remove(folder, name)


def to_temporary(folder, name, read, max_bytes, pending, handles, mode=0o644):
    """Copy everything read() returns into a fresh scratch file next to the destination, hashing along the way and
    stopping at the cap; return (scratch name, SHA-256, open handle). The name is added to pending before the file
    is created and its identity noted right after; the handle goes into handles. That lets the caller's cleanup
    delete and close both after a failure at any point, and keeping the handle open lets the rename step confirm
    that the file it installs is this one."""
    temporary = temporary_name(name)
    entry = [temporary, None]
    pending.append(entry)
    handle = os.open(temporary, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL | os.O_NOFOLLOW, 0o600, dir_fd=folder)
    handles.append(handle)
    entry[1] = identity(os.fstat(handle))
    hasher, size = hashlib.sha256(), 0
    while chunk := read(CHUNK):
        size += len(chunk)
        if size > max_bytes:
            raise TooLarge(f"the bytes pass the cap of {max_bytes}")
        hasher.update(chunk)
        view = memoryview(chunk)
        while view:
            view = view[os.write(handle, view):]
    os.fsync(handle)
    os.fchmod(handle, mode)
    return temporary, hasher.hexdigest(), handle


def same_file(info, other):
    return identity(info) == identity(other)


def commit(deadline, interrupts, state):
    """Make the placement final. The checked file is already at the destination; hold interrupts, switch the
    deadline off, then mark the run placed with a single assignment. The cleanup consults that mark: without it the
    placement is undone, with it the destination is kept, so only events before the assignment can reverse it."""
    interrupts.hold()
    deadline.disarm()
    state.placed = True


def discard(folder, pending):
    """Remove every pending temporary file now (a copy that did not verify), not at the end; an entry leaves the
    list only once its removal succeeded, so a failure here is retried and named by the final cleanup."""
    while pending:
        temporary, own = pending[0]
        remove_own(folder, temporary, own)
        del pending[0]


def clear_destination(folder, name, pending, cleared):
    """After a refusal, remove from the destination a file this run renamed there (one whose identity a pending
    entry holds), only while the name still holds it, so exit 1 never leaves a file written. If the refusal came
    before the destination was cleared (set aside, or removed where it stood), whatever file is there was never
    verified, so it is removed as it stands; a folder is never removed. It is called only for a run that was not
    placed."""
    owns = [own for _, own in pending if own is not None]
    if cleared and not owns:
        return
    try:
        info = os.stat(name, dir_fd=folder, follow_symlinks=False)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return
    if stat.S_ISDIR(info.st_mode):
        return
    if not cleared or identity(info) in owns:
        remove(folder, name)


def settle(folder, temporary, handle, found, digest, name):
    """Rename the temporary file onto the destination only when its digest matches, and only when the name still
    holds the file this helper wrote and hashed, before and after the rename."""
    if found != digest:
        return False
    written = os.fstat(handle)
    if not same_file(os.stat(temporary, dir_fd=folder, follow_symlinks=False), written):
        raise Refused("the temporary file was replaced before it was placed")
    os.rename(temporary, name, src_dir_fd=folder, dst_dir_fd=folder)
    if not same_file(os.stat(name, dir_fd=folder, follow_symlinks=False), written):
        remove(folder, name)
        raise Refused("the destination was replaced while it was placed")
    return True


def set_aside(folder, name, pending):
    """Move whatever occupies the destination to a scratch name with a single atomic rename, before reading any of it.
    This is the program's first change there; afterward the name is vacant or holds a checked file at all times,
    process death included. If the rename fails, the file is removed where it stands, or the refusal lists it; a
    failure earlier in the run has the cleanup delete it in the same way. Returns the pending entry, or None if
    nothing was there. A folder is moved back and the run refused, because it cannot contain downloaded bytes and
    deleting a directory tree is out of scope; a link or device is refused and left for the cleanup to delete."""
    entry = [temporary_name(name), None]
    pending.append(entry)
    try:
        os.rename(name, entry[0], src_dir_fd=folder, dst_dir_fd=folder)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        pending.remove(entry)
        return None
    except OSError:  # it cannot be set aside, so it is removed where it stands, or the refusal names it
        pending.remove(entry)
        remove(folder, name)
        return None
    info = os.stat(entry[0], dir_fd=folder, follow_symlinks=False)
    entry[1] = identity(info)
    if stat.S_ISDIR(info.st_mode):
        os.rename(entry[0], name, src_dir_fd=folder, dst_dir_fd=folder)
        pending.remove(entry)
        raise Refused("the destination changed during the run into a folder")
    if not stat.S_ISREG(info.st_mode):
        raise Refused("the destination changed during the run into something other than a regular file")
    return entry


def place(url, digest, folder, name, open_url, max_bytes, deadline=None, state=None, interrupts=None):
    """All file work here uses the folder handle; the path is not consulted again. An existing destination is moved
    away first, then copied to a scratch file and hashed as that copy, so a good result never depends on reading a
    name twice. The deadline remains armed until the placement is confirmed. A single cleanup is responsible for
    every scratch file and for anything this run put at the destination, deleting each only while its name still
    refers to that file. It runs with interrupts held and reads the placed mark, so once a run is marked placed, no
    later error removes the destination."""
    deadline = deadline or Deadline()
    state = state if state is not None else Outcome()
    interrupts = interrupts if interrupts is not None else Interrupts()
    pending, handles, failure, cleared, deferred = [], [], None, False, None
    try:
        deadline.arm()
        aside = set_aside(folder, name, pending)
        cleared = True  # the name is empty now, or its file is set aside under a temporary name
        if aside is not None:
            found = None
            try:
                handle = os.open(aside[0], os.O_RDONLY | os.O_NOFOLLOW | os.O_NONBLOCK, dir_fd=folder)
            except OSError:
                handle = None  # it cannot be read, so it cannot be verified: it goes like any other wrong file
        if aside is not None and handle is not None:
            try:
                found_info = os.fstat(handle)
                if identity(found_info) != aside[1] or not stat.S_ISREG(found_info.st_mode):
                    raise Refused("the destination changed during the run")
                mode = stat.S_IMODE(found_info.st_mode)  # the copy keeps the existing file's mode bits
                try:
                    temporary, found, copy = to_temporary(folder, name, lambda size: os.read(handle, size),
                                                          max_bytes, pending, handles, mode)
                except TooLarge:
                    found = None  # over the cap, so it is not the pinned file
            finally:
                close_quietly(handle)
        if aside is not None:
            if found == digest:
                remove_own(folder, aside[0], aside[1])  # the verified copy replaces it, so the original goes first
                pending.remove(aside)
                if settle(folder, temporary, copy, found, digest, name):
                    commit(deadline, interrupts, state)
                    return
            discard(folder, pending)  # the file set aside and its copy did not verify: both go before the fetch
        if open_url is None:
            opener = https_only_opener()

            def open_url(address):
                return opener.open(address, timeout=TIMEOUT_SECONDS)
        try:
            with open_url(url) as body:
                temporary, found, written = to_temporary(folder, name, body.read, max_bytes, pending, handles)
        except (OSError, http.client.HTTPException, urllib.error.URLError, ValueError) as error:
            raise Refused(f"the fetch failed: {error}") from None
        if not settle(folder, temporary, written, found, digest, name):
            raise Refused(f"digest mismatch: expected {digest}, got {found}")
        commit(deadline, interrupts, state)
    except BaseException as error:
        failure = error
        raise
    finally:
        # Nothing may stop this cleanup early, so interrupts are held and the deadline switched off before anything
        # else. Signals arrive between bytecodes; these repeated attempts catch the one interrupt and the one alarm
        # that might still land here, while an exception already propagating keeps propagating.
        try:
            interrupts.hold()
            deadline.disarm()
        except (Exception, KeyboardInterrupt):
            pass
        except BaseException as error:  # not the cleanup's to drop: raised once every step has run
            deferred = deferred or error
        try:
            interrupts.hold()
            deadline.disarm()
        except (Exception, KeyboardInterrupt):
            pass
        except BaseException as error:  # not the cleanup's to drop: raised once every step has run
            deferred = deferred or error
        try:
            interrupts.hold()
            deadline.disarm()
        except (Exception, KeyboardInterrupt):
            pass
        except BaseException as error:  # not the cleanup's to drop: raised once every step has run
            deferred = deferred or error
        # Each step below is attempted regardless of how the previous ones went.
        try:
            signal.setitimer(signal.ITIMER_REAL, 0)
        except Exception:
            pass
        for handle in handles:
            try:
                close_quietly(handle)
            except Exception:
                pass
        left = []
        for temporary, own in pending:  # only leftovers this run made, each while it still holds that file
            try:
                remove_own(folder, temporary, own)
            except Exception:
                left.append(temporary)
        if not state.placed:  # undo an unfinished placement, or remove a file the run never got to move away
            try:
                clear_destination(folder, name, pending, cleared)
            except Exception:
                left.append(name)
        # Exit 1 never hides a leftover: the refusal names it, after the reason the run failed, if any. A placed run has
        # renamed every pending name away, so a failure while checking one leaves nothing and refuses nothing.
        if deferred is not None:
            raise deferred
        if left and not state.placed:
            reason = f" after: {failure}" if failure is not None else ""
            raise Refused(f"could not remove {', '.join(left)}{reason}") from failure


def shown(text):
    """Render text as one printable ASCII line, writing backslashes, control characters and non-ASCII as escapes."""
    return "".join(c if " " <= c <= "~" and c != "\\" else c.encode("unicode_escape").decode("ascii")
                   for c in str(text))


def say(text, stream):
    """The one place the helper prints, so no message can skip the escaping."""
    print(shown(text), file=stream)


def described(error):
    """An exception's text, or its type's name when even making the text fails, so a report never raises."""
    try:
        return str(error)
    except Exception:
        return type(error).__name__


def last_resort(kind, value, trace):
    """Handle an exception that gets past main, such as an interrupt during a report. Python's default output would
    include chained text without escaping, so this prints a single escaped line naming only the exception type. It
    never raises, because if it did Python would fall back to its full default output. The status is still nonzero:
    1 in general, 130 for an interrupt."""
    try:
        say(f"fetch-pinned: refused: {getattr(kind, '__name__', 'error')}", sys.stderr)
    except BaseException:
        pass


def refusal(error):
    """(exit status, the one line) for an exception that ended a run before its commit point."""
    if isinstance(error, Malformed):
        return 2, f"fetch-pinned: malformed: {described(error)}"
    if isinstance(error, Refused):
        return 1, f"fetch-pinned: refused: {described(error)}"
    if isinstance(error, OSError):  # any operating-system failure is a refusal; nothing unverified is left in place
        return 1, f"fetch-pinned: refused: {error.strerror or described(error)}"
    return 1, f"fetch-pinned: refused: {type(error).__name__}: {described(error)}"  # an interrupt included


def main(argv, interrupts=None):
    """Run once and return the exit status; a report that cannot be printed never changes it, and a failure after
    placement keeps exit 0 and is printed as one warning line. interrupts is the
    executable's holder, kept held until the process exits. Called in process without one, main takes over
    interrupts for the run only and puts the handler back before it returns, dropping one it held, and it sets no
    last-resort report."""
    if len(argv) != 4:
        try:
            say("fetch-pinned: usage: fetch-pinned <https-url> <sha256> <destination>", sys.stderr)
        except BaseException:
            pass
        return 2
    holder = interrupts if interrupts is not None else Interrupts()
    outcome = Outcome()
    try:
        if interrupts is None:
            holder.install()
        late = None
        try:
            fetch_pinned(argv[1], argv[2], argv[3], outcome=outcome, interrupts=holder)
        except BaseException as error:
            late = error
            if not outcome.placed:  # before the commit point: rolled back, so the run is refused
                status = 1
                try:
                    status, line = refusal(error)
                    say(line, sys.stderr)
                except BaseException:
                    pass
                return status
        try:  # past the commit point the file is verified and in place, so even a report that fails is exit 0
            say(f"fetch-pinned: placed {argv[3]}", sys.stdout)
        except BaseException:
            pass
        if late is not None and not isinstance(late, KeyboardInterrupt):  # after placement: reported, not swallowed
            try:
                say(f"fetch-pinned: warning: {type(late).__name__}: {described(late)}", sys.stderr)
            except BaseException:
                pass
        return 0
    finally:
        if interrupts is None:
            try:
                holder.release()
            except Exception:
                holder.release()


def command(argv):
    """The executable's entry, and the one place that changes process-wide state, since the process ends with the
    run: it sets the last-resort report and holds interrupts from the commit point until the process exits."""
    sys.excepthook = last_resort
    interrupts = Interrupts()
    interrupts.install()
    return main(argv, interrupts)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(command(sys.argv))
